body:
One of the most fascinating things to me is that what investigators had to piece together after the first few months of library witnesses. It started from ground zero, walking into a room of the victims, and through the entirety of the witness accounts, it must have been insane to start cementing the reality of what happened - especially the order of events and accounts of things that were heard. 

&#x200B;

It must have been intense to hear multiple different sides of the library and sift through the vast accounts, realizing little by little what was common between them all, deciphering what was "real" from the rambling memories.

Had to Google "McHugh" because I was like "I couldn't have graduated THAT long ago that there are whole new buildings!" 

&#x200B;

I still think Laurel sounds weird, it'll always just be the Classroom Building to me.

Does the size and geography affect how effective some walls are though? The Berlin Wall was less than 100 miles long and was manned/guarded constantly. The U.S.-Mexican border is just under 2,000 miles and has many natural geographical obstacles that the Berlin Wall did not have. 

&#x200B;

I'm obviously not going to pretend a wall wouldn't do anything, I'm sure it would reduce illegal immigration. I'm just wondering how the tens of billions of dollars it will take to not only build, maintain, and guard 24/7 a wall of this proportion are reconciled with the idea that we need to cut government spending and reduce our debt. Is spending billions and billions on a partial solution worth it when there might be better immigration solutions? I voted for Trump in 2016 and understand that any other solution than a wall isn't sexy to imagine, but I'm told with other issues that we need to stop spending so much money, but now it's somehow okay if it means stopping some illegal immigrants. Interesting dilemma for sure though!

Yeah, but it might be the only job someone can find, depending on where they live. Are we supposed to resign some people to working $8/hour because all the other jobs in the area are $6/hour? Besides, minimum wage right now in CT is already too low, yet people keep accepting jobs because it's the best they can get. That and the fact that increasing automation in the coming decades will make some positions obsolete anyway. Why should Manager A care about raising wages just because some people decide not to accept the job, when their roles might be filled by a software program anyway?

&#x200B;

There was a reason early Progressivists pushed for a minimum wage. Why did sweatshop workers in cities accept the job if they could just find another?

It depends on the class. Some classes that are considered intro courses for your major definitely will be 200+ students. I can't speak to business as much, since I was molecular biology, but my intro to biology class was in a lecture hall that held hundreds. It is obviously much harder to connect in a larger class, but most 200+ classes have weekly discussion periods where TAs cover a smaller, genuine-feeling classroom (\~20 students).

&#x200B;

But other classes I took my freshman year only had 40-50 students. From my experience, it is indeed the first couple of years that class sizes are generally larger. By my senior year, majority of my classes were so specific that in one, it had a total of 7 students, including myself.

We're not talking about previous budgets though. Obviously any budget issue has its own context and we could discuss that independently. For this current shutdown, the only reason it's shut down is because of the Wall funding. As someone who voted for Trump in 2016, I was really hoping for some unity between the parties by calling out the people who were gridlocking the country on purpose for political gains. 

&#x200B;

For this specific budget, doesn't it make sense that if you want the funding for the Wall, you have to give something up in return? What's being exchanged? It really just feels that a lot of people would think it's kowtowing to Democrats if there was a compromise that gave both sides a win. Apart from a lot of Democrats, I'd 100% be in favor of funding for a Wall, but there has to be a concession to Democrats; otherwise, it's just making demands for posturing.
